WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.180
Although they’re often electric, on-screen
kisses aren’t always planned.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.440
Actors can surprise their co-stars with a
kiss during a scene for any number of reasons.

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.580
From the tender to the funny to the deeply
controversial, here are a few on-screen kisses

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.300
that were never supposed to happen.

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:18.000
Found in translation

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:23.100
One of Lost in Translation’s most iconic
scenes revolves around the tender moment shared

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.760
at the end of the movie between Scarlett Johansson’s
Charlotte and Bill Murray’s character Bob.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:31.720
During filming, Murray famously whispered
something in Johansson's ear that neither

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.800
actor has ever revealed.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.860
The ensuing kiss wasn’t planned, either.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.660
When asked about the scene, director Sofia
Coppola told The Daily Beast,

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.560
"I remember sometimes [Murray] would spring
things on [Johansson], and it was fun to get

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:51.260
her reaction."

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.480
Sparking anger

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.960
During Stranger Things 2’s season finale,
Lucas and newcomer Max share a kiss at their

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.080
school’s winter dance.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.290
In an episode of the aftershow series Beyond
Stranger Things, Sadie Sink — who played

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:08.720
Max — explained that the kiss was a surprise
and that showrunners Matt and Ross Duffer

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.320
sprang it on her the day of filming.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.320
"Oh, Sadie, are you ready for the kiss?

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:12.820
I was like, what?

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.960
No, that's not in the script, that's not happening!"

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:21.100
Ross Duffer had previously joked with Sink
about her kissing her co-star Caleb McLaughlin

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:25.280
, and the idea had stressed her out so much
that he decided to have it happen for real.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:28.140
It was a decision which sparked outrage online.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:31.880
In the wake of the controversy, Sink told
TheWrap that she had “always felt comfortable”

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:32.880
on set, saying

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:35.700
“[The Duffer Brothers] always create a comfortable
space.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.680
And if I felt uncomfortable with anything,
I wouldn’t have done it.”

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.760
"Anyways, it's great, I can't imagine it without
it, thank you!"

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:42.820
"Yeah… you're welcome."

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.040
A last kiss

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:49.880
Luke and Leia’s scene towards the end of
The Last Jedi is arguably one of the movie’s

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:50.780
finest moments.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:55.180
It’s an exchange which carries extra weight
considering Carrie Fisher’s tragic passing

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:56.580
only months after filming ended.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.119
The scene is punctuated by a forehead kiss
from Luke to Leia which was improvised on

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:02.119
Mark Hamill’s part.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.540
"It was just spontaneous.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:10.780
And, a really momentous for me, because Luke
was saying goodbye to his sister forever."

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:14.049
Hamill has been unable to watch the scene
since.

00:02:14.049 --> 00:02:18.060
“She’ll be forever missed, and she’s
irreplaceable.”

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:20.560
Woody's crush

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.020
After the home release of The Hunger Games:
Mockingjay – Part 2, Woody Harrelson told

00:02:25.029 --> 00:02:28.859
Entertainment Tonight that his on-screen kiss
with Elizabeth Banks had nothing to do with

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:29.380
the script.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.099
"She didn't know I was even going to do it.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:32.900
I just kissed her."

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.620
Harrelson later told the Associated Press,

00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:38.069
“I, personally, always wanted to kiss her.

00:02:38.069 --> 00:02:39.780
I don't know if that was the right thing to
do."

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:44.019
Although Harrelson denied knowing it, the
AP’s reporter claimed she'd heard that Banks

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:48.220
intentionally flubbed takes during the scene
so she could keep locking lips with Harrelson.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.999
Krakens, snakes, and stunt doubles

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:55.239
Thanks to the logistical realities of filming
a television show, an unplanned kiss between

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.690
two actors can often involve more people than
initially intended.

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:04.180
That’s what happened after Game of Thrones
actors Gemma Whelan and Indira Varma improvised

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.400
a kiss between their characters Yara Greyjoy
and Ellaria Sand.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:08.660
“Leave him be.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.220
Seems like you don't need a protector.”

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.840
This moment takes place on a ship just prior
to an attack by antagonist Euron Greyjoy.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.930
Whelan's back was injured at the time, however,
and since she couldn't perform all of her

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:29.760
stunts, her double was brought in to replace
her — and also had to kiss Varma.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.800
Varma told Entertainment Weekly that

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:35.540
“I had to start kissing this poor stunt
double and she was so terrified.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:38.120
I don't think she'd ever been put in that
situation before.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.299
She's used to falling over and being attacked
and all the stunts, but to be kissed by an

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:44.040
actress was a bit beyond her."

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:46.320
Pratt finds a way

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.940
According to Bryce Dallas Howard, the kiss
between her character Claire and Chris Pratt’s

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.360
Owen was never in the script for Jurassic
World.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:54.660
She told Out Magazine,

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:58.040
"We had shot the scene in the way that it
was written — there was no kiss.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.160
And then on the last day, that happened — what
you see in the movie."

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:06.260
The movie’s director, Colin Trevorrow, however,
has since revealed that it wasn’t entirely

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:06.840
spontaneous.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:10.380
He took Pratt aside and suggested he kiss
Howard, but never told her.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.839
On Jurassic World’s commentary, he explained:

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.720
"The idea was we're just going to have Chris
Pratt surprise [Howard] in front of 200 people,

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:19.260
and that's the take that's in the movie."

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:21.560
Office romance

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.360
During the season 3 premiere of The Office,
Steve Carell’s character Michael learns

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:29.660
that his employee Oscar — played by Oscar
Nunez — is gay.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.240
In a misguided attempt to prove how tolerant
he is… this happens.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:46.320
According to Nuñez, however, the script only
called for Carell to hug him.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:47.540
He told the AV Club,

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.420
"We were just supposed to hug, and he kept
hugging me.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:54.880
And that particular take he came in really
close, and I'm like, 'Where is he going with

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:55.420
this?'"

